*Confidentiality - Protects information from unauthorized disclosure

Snooping - Involves gathering information that is left out in the open
(protection) clean desk policy

Dumpster Diving - Intruder looking through trash to try to recover sensitive documents
(protection) paper shredding

Eavedropping - Someone positions themselves to listen to secret conversations 
(protection) closed room meetings 

Wiretapping - When attacker gains access to a network and monitors the data
(protection) Encryption

Social Engineering - Attacker uses psychological tricks to persuade employee to gain access to internal systems
(protection) Training and Education for users

*Integrity - Protects information from unauthorized changes

Unauthorized modification - occurs when attacker makes changes without permission 
(protection) least privelage 

Impersonation - attacker pretends to be someone else
(protection) user education

Man in the middle - the attacker is placed in the middle of communications
(protection) encryption

Replay - attacker eavesdrops on logins and reuse the captured credentials
(protection) encryption

*Availability - Ensures information to authorized users remains available when needed

Denial of Service (DoS) - Attacker bomars network or system with overwhelming amount of traffic
(protection) Firewalls

Power Outages - An event that occurs after a disruption to the system causing it to lose power
(protection) Back up generators

Hardware Failures - When the hardware components of computer fail and disrupt access to information 
(protection) back up components

Identification - Making a claim of identity 
(Claiming Identity)

Authentication - Proving a claim of identity 
(Verifying Identity)

Authorization - Ensures an action is allowed 
(Granting Access) 

Accountability - Tracking and recording users activities for auditing, compliance, or billing purposes
(Tracking Identity) 

Multifactor Authentication - Combines two different authentication factors
- Something you KNOW: Passwords, Pins, Questions
- Something you ARE: Biometrics, Fingerprint, Face Recognition
- Something you HAVE: Key Phob, Token 

Non Repudiation - Prevents someone from denying the truth 
ex; Signed contract, Signatures

(PII) Personal Identifiable Info - all information that can be tied back to a specific person 

(PHI) Protected Health Info - Health care info 
ex; HIPPA

*Risk Management - The process of identifying, assessing, and mitigating potential threats in an organization

Risk assessment - indentifies and evaluates risk 

Threats - external forces that jepoardize security 

Threat Vector - methods used by attackers

Vulnerabilities - weaknesses in your security controls 

Risks - a combination of a threat and a vulnerability 

Likelihood - the probabilty a risk will occur

Impact - the amount of damage a risk will cause 

Qualitive Risk Assessment - Uses subjective ratings to evaluate risk, likelihood, and impact

Quantitative Risk Assessment - Uses objective numeric ratings to evaulate risk likelihood and impact

Quantitative Risk Analysis - This is used to calculate the Single Loss Expectancy. The estimated monetary loss from a 
single occurence of a specific risk event

(AV x EF = SLE)

(AV) Asset Value - The dollar value assigned to an asset, including both direct costs and indirect costs
- Direct Costs (purchase price)
- Indirect Costs (downtime, data loss, regulatory fines)

(EF) Exposure Factor - The percentage of the asset's value that would be lost or damaged if the threat were realized.
It os expressed as a decimal 
( 100% loss = 1,00, 50% loss = 0.50) 

(SLE) Single Loss Expectancy - The total estimated loss from a single incident calculated by multiplying AV by EF 

Subject - A subject is an entity that initiates an action or request to access a resource.
- A user 
- A process or application
- A device 

Object - An object is the resource that the subject wants to access. It is passive - it is the target of the subjects action.
- Files
- Documents
- Databases
- Applications
- Network Devices
- Hardware or Software Assets

Risk Treatment - Analyzes and implements possible responses to control risk
- Transfer
- Avoid 
- Mitigate 
- Remediate 

Risk Avoidance - Changes business practices to make a risk irrelevant

Risk Mitigation - Reduces the likelihood or impact of a risk

Risk Acceptance - The choice to continue operations in the face of a risk

Risk Profile - Set of risks that it faces

Risk Tolerance - The level of risk an organization is willing to accept

Segregation of Duties - Allocating tasks and associated privileges for a business process to multiple
staff members to ensure that no single person has control over all aspects of a critical transaction or process
(No single person control) 

*Security Controls - Reduce the likelihood or impact of a risk and help identify issues 

Defense of Depth - uses overlapping security controls 

Preventive Controls - stop a security issue from occuring 
ex; Hardening

Detective Controls - identify security issues requiring investigation 
ex; Virus Scans

Recovery Controls - Remediate security issues that have occured 
ex; Backups

Technical Controls - Use technology to achieve control objectives 

Administrative Controls - Use processes to achieve control objectives 

Physical Controls - Impact the physical world 

Configuration Management - Tracks specific device settings

Baselines - Provide a configuration snapshot

Verisoning - Assigns numbers to each version

*Security Governance 

GDPR: General Data Protection Regulation (European Union)

PCI DSS - A private regulation governing credit card information 

*Security Policy Framework

Policies - Describe an organizations security expectations

Standards - Describe specific security controls

Guidelines - Describe best practices (advice)

Procedures - Step by step instructions 

(AUP) Acceptible Use Policies - Describe authorized uses of technology 

Data handling policies - Describe how to protect sensitive information 

Password policies - Cover password security practices

(BYOD) Bring Your Own Device policy - cover use of personal devices with company information

Privacy policies - Cover the use of personally identifiable information

Change management policy - Cover the documentation, approval , and rollback of technology changes 

*Business Continuity Planning - the set of controls designed to keep a business running in the face of adversity, 
whether natural or man-made

Business Continuity Phases:

1. Analysis - The initial phase that involves idenifying potential risks and assesing their impact on business operations. 
This analysis helps organizations understand vulnerabilities and prioritize their response efforts

2. Planning - Planning 


High Availabilty - Uses multiple systems to protect against service failure 
(a cluster or pair of something for redundacy)

Fault Tolerance - makes a single system resilient against technical failures
(RAID)

Load balancing - Spreads demand across systems

(PDUs) Managing Power Distribution Units - Provide power cleaning and management for a rack

*Incident Response - Provide structure during cybersecurity incidents. 
(They describe the policies and procedures governing cybersecurity incidents)

Incident Response process phases - Preparation --> Detection & Analysis --> 
Containment, Eradication, and Recovery --> Post Incident Activity 

Patch Management Lifecycle - Asset Discovery --> Vulnerability Assessment --> Patch Acquisition -->
Patch Validation --> Patch Deployment --> Reporting

IRP elements: Statement of purpose
               Strategies and goals for incident response
               Approach to incident response
               Communication with other groups 
               Senior leadership approval

(SLA) Service Level Agreement - A contract between a provider and a customer that includes provisions
around performance, availability, security , response times, and accountability 

Need To Know - Access to a resource should only be granted to a user who requires access to the resource
in order to perform a business function 

(SIEM) Security Incident and Event Management - Security solution that collects information from diverse sources, analyzes 
it for signs for security incidents and retains it for later us

*Disaster Recovery - Restores normal operations as quickly as possible

(RTO) Recovery Time Objective - The amount of time to restore service

(RPO) Recovery Point Objective -The amount of data recovered

(RSL) Recovery Service Level - The percentage of service to restore 

Disaster recovery sites - Provide alternate data processing 

Hot site - An exact, fully equipped replica of a business primary computer and data center systems

Cold site - Basic backup location that provides essential infrastructure like power, cooling, and internet
but no active computer hardware

Warm site - In between hot and cold site

*Logical Access Controls - Digital safety mechanisms that regulate who gets access to computer networks or data 

Job rotation - Moves employees through different positions

Provisioning - After onboarding, administration create authentication credentials and grant appropiate authorization

Deprovionsing - During the offboarding process, administrators disable accounts and revoke authorizations at the 
appropiate time 

Routine workflow - Disables accounts on a scheduled basis for planned departures

Authorization - (Tracking Identity) Determines what an authenticated user can do

Least Privelage - User should have minumum set of permissions necessary to perform their job

(MAC) Mandatory Access Control - Permissions are determined by the hierarchy and security level (military)

(DAC) Discretionary Access Control - Permissions are determined by file owners

(RBAC) Role-Based Access Control - Grants permissions based on specific roles

(ABAC) Attribute-Based Access Control - Grants permissions based on attributes 

(BIA) Business Impact Analysis - A systematic process that identifies critical business functions and 
evaluates the potential consequences of disruptions to help organizations prioritize recovery and continuity efforts.

Access Control Matrix - A table containing a set of subjects, objects, and permissions used by systems to implement rules, 
define access control permissions, and enforce DAC

The capabilities list - a structured set of defined abilities, functions, or competencies that an organization, team, or 
individual must have to effectively protect information, systems, and infrastructure from threats. 






















